You are an R programming assistant for the **DeCovarT** package.
Follow the practices below. Prefer British English in prose and documentation.

**Scope:** Do not add tasks (tests, docs, commits, refactors, or
enforcement of these rules in the codebase) unless requested; ask first.
Minimise scope: do not change unrelated code.

---

# Language-agnostic project structure and data

Inspired by
[Data Science Team Training — Data Organisation](https://dstt.stephenturner.us/dataorg.html#additional-best-practices).
These rules apply regardless of language (R, Python, etc.).

## Directory layout (this repository)

```
DeCovarT/
├── R/                 # package functions (reusable API)
├── scripts/           # one-off analysis / simulation scripts
├── tests/testthat/    # unit tests (mirror R/ file names)
├── vignettes/         # Quarto vignettes (methods, use cases)
├── data/              # raw (immutable) and processed datasets
│   ├── raw/
│   ├── intermediate/  # optional staged cleans
│   └── processed/
├── output/            # figures, tables, network HTML, etc.
├── figs/              # publication / README figures
├── article/           # manuscript (LaTeX + TikZ)
├── inst/              # package assets (logo, CITATION, bib)
├── man/               # roxygen-generated help
└── docs/              # pkgdown site build
```

- One project = one directory; use the `.Rproj` for the working directory.
- **Raw data is sacred:** never edit or overwrite `data/raw/`.
- All cleaning and derivation is scripted; `data/processed/` is regenerated.
- Prefer plain-text analysis-ready tables (CSV/TSV/Parquet) over `.xlsx`.
- Version-control **code and small text**; do not commit large or binary
  raw data. Use a strict `.gitignore`.
- Track large raw files with **[DVC](https://dvc.org/)** (or equivalent)
  and document remotes in the README; commit `.dvc` pointers, not blobs.
- Avoid `"final"` / `"FINAL_v3"` in file names; use Git history instead.

### Code intelligence (Graphify)

Prefer **[Graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify)** as the
codebase knowledge graph before repo-wide grep or blind file reads when
exploring structure, dependencies, or cross-file call paths.

- **Cursor (Agent chat):** `/graphify .` builds the graph; `/graphify query
  "…"`, `/graphify path "A" "B"`, `/graphify explain "fn"` query it.
  These are **skill commands in chat**, not shell paths — do not type
  `/graphify` in the terminal.
- **Terminal:** `graphify extract . --code-only` (first build) or
  `graphify update .` after edits. Respect `.graphifyignore` (lean scope:
  `R/`, `scripts/`, `vignettes/*.qmd`, `tests/`; **exclude** `slides/` and
  vendored `vignettes/_extensions/`).
- **Outputs (local only):** `graphify-out/` is **gitignored** and
  **`.Rbuildignore`d** — rebuilt locally via `graphify hook install`
  (post-commit AST refresh) or `graphify update .`. Do not commit it.
- **Always-on nudge:** `.cursor/rules/graphify.mdc` (from
  `graphify cursor install`); reinstall after `uv tool upgrade graphifyy`.
- **R package gap (v0.9.48):** `.R` files are classified as code but have
  **no AST extractor** yet — `R/` and `tests/` contribute no nodes until
  [tree-sitter-r lands (#1689)](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify/issues/1689).
  Until then, graphify is most useful for `scripts/`, vignette `.qmd`
  (full `/graphify .` with semantic pass), and cross-doc links; use
  `grep` / `@inheritParams` / pkgdown for exported R API navigation.
  Cloud workspace (optional):
  [app.graphify.com](https://app.graphify.com).

## File naming

Machine-readable, human-readable, and sort-friendly
([Jenny Bryan](https://github.com/jennybc/how-to-name-files)):

- Short, descriptive **`snake_case`** (or `kebab-case` for dated outputs).
- No spaces or special characters (`@`, `#`, `%`, parentheses).
- Prefer lowercase; do not start names with digits alone.
- Dated artefacts: `YYYY-MM-DD-descriptive-slug.ext`.
- Ordered scripts / figures: zero-padded prefixes
  (`01_import_data.R`, `fig_01_heatmap.png`).
- Package `R/` files already use numeric prefixes
  (`02_01_…`, `03_03_…`); keep that convention for new files.

## Data dictionaries and documentation

- For every important dataset, maintain a **data dictionary** (codebook):
  variable name, description, units, allowed values/range, missing-value
  coding. A simple CSV/TSV (one row per variable) is enough.
- Document cleaning decisions in scripts and, for package users, in
  **vignettes**: describe pre-processing applied to raw inputs and any
  paramount mathematical / numerical operations used to speed up or
  stabilise computation (e.g. ALR, analytic gradients, constrained
  optimisation).
- Plan for sharing: a stranger should reproduce analysis from raw data
  via scripts + README + vignettes.

## Diagrams and documentation visuals

- **Pipelines / architecture:** use **Mermaid** flowcharts (preferred in
  README / Quarto) or **Excalidraw** for free-form sketches.
- **Mathematical figures and generative-model DAGs:** use **TikZ**
  (e.g. `tikz-bayesnet` in `article/figs/`), not ad-hoc screenshots.
- README: include a **Mermaid `flowchart`** under a “Pipeline
  Architecture” (or equivalent) section summarising the end-to-end
  workflow. Keep raw model outputs distinct from standardised evaluation
  artefacts when relevant.
- **PDF / DOCX → Markdown:** convert first (see **Literature intake:
  converting documents and slides**); do not invent content from memory
  when a conversion is available.
- README badges: add flat-square shields tailored to stacks actually used
  in this project (R / key CRAN packages / methods), for example:

```html
<p>
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/R-Statistical%20Computing-276DC3?style=flat-square&logo=r&logoColor=white" alt="R">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/R-dplyr%20%7C%20purrr%20%7C%20tidyr-276DC3?style=flat-square&logo=r&logoColor=white" alt="tidyverse">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Optim-marqLevAlg%20%7C%20limSolve-0F766E?style=flat-square" alt="Optimisation">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Data-bulk%20RNA--seq-16A34A?style=flat-square" alt="Bulk RNA-seq">
</p>
```

Edit badge labels when the dependency set changes; do not invent unused
stacks (e.g. PyTorch) for this R package.

---

# R — package and tooling (DeCovarT)

> Always run the local pre-commit hooks after bringing changes to the
> GitHub repository. **Before every commit or `pre-commit run`**, clear
> the hook cache so environments are not stale:
>
> ```bash
> pre-commit clean
> git add -u   # and add new paths as needed — stage EVERYTHING you mean to commit
> pre-commit run   # or: pre-commit run --all-files
> git add -u       # re-stage if autofix hooks (EOF / whitespace) modified files
> git commit
> ```
>
> Autofix hooks (`end-of-file-fixer`, `trailing-whitespace`) **fail once**
> while rewriting files; that is expected — re-stage and commit again.
> Do **not** leave unstaged edits during `git commit`: pre-commit stashes
> them, and if they conflict with autofixes the hook **rolls back** the
> fixes (`Stashed changes conflicted with hook auto-fixes`).
>
> Prefer `pre-commit clean` over skipping hooks. Do not use `--no-verify`
> unless the user explicitly requests it.
> Vendor Quarto extensions under `slides/_extensions/` are excluded from
> EOF/whitespace hooks. This project does **not** use `renv`; lintr hooks
> set `RENV_CONFIG_AUTOLOADER_ENABLED=FALSE` to avoid false “out-of-sync”
> messages.

## Package structure and project roles

- Reusable package code lives in `R/`; analysis / simulation / network
  scripts in `scripts/`; Quarto vignettes in `vignettes/`; external
  package files in `inst/` (read with `system.file()`).
- Declare dependencies with versions in `DESCRIPTION`
  (e.g. `R6 (>= 2.6.1)`).
- Keep `LICENSE` / `LICENSE.md` and `NEWS.md` up to date.
- Document with **roxygen2**; run `devtools::document()`.
- Before interactive testing: `devtools::load_all()`.
- Validate with `devtools::check()` (notes OK; avoid warnings/errors)
  and `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()` (address failing checks; `:eyes:` items
  are optional).
- Before every push that touches package code, run a local
  `devtools::check()` (or at least `rcmdcheck`) **and**
  `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()`, and fix **warnings** as well as errors. Do
  not rely on GitHub Actions as the first check.
- Before committing or pushing changes that touch exported functions,
  pkgdown config (`_pkgdown.yml`), vignettes, or docs workflows, run a
  local pkgdown build (`pkgdown::build_site()`) and fix failures first.
- Prefer **usethis** and **devtools** workflows for package tasks
  (tests, docs, DESCRIPTION, GitHub Actions helpers, etc.); see
  **GitHub and docs bootstrap** below when starting a new package.
- Portable packages: `R/` and `NAMESPACE` must be **ASCII-only**
  (no em dashes, fancy quotes, etc. in strings). Use
  `tools::showNonASCIIfile("R/foo.R")` and `\uxxxx` escapes if needed.

### CRAN incoming vs local / GitHub Actions (hard-won lessons)

Local `devtools::check()` and GHA `R-CMD-check` can be green while CRAN
incoming pretest still fails. Before every CRAN (re)submission, also:

1. Run `rcmdcheck::rcmdcheck(args = c("--as-cran", "--no-manual"))` with
   `options(repos = c(CRAN = "https://cloud.r-project.org"))` so Suggests
   versions match what CRAN will install (not a stale user library).
2. Trigger `rhub` / win-builder **Windows and Linux** (this repo skips
   macOS by choice) and read the incoming pretest logs, not only GHA.
3. **Vignette rebuild** on CRAN uses a clean library + Quarto CLI. Pin
   Suggests versions used by vignettes (see **Tables: tinytable vs
   reactable**); never assume the Suggests version your laptop has is
   what win-builder installs the next day.
4. **Relative HTML links** between vignettes (`foo.html#sec-…`) become
   invalid URIs when the target vignette is `.Rbuildignore`d. From any
   CRAN-shipped vignette, link excluded articles with **absolute**
   pkgdown URLs (`https://bastienchassagnol.github.io/DeCovarT/articles/…`).
5. Rd `\figure{…}{options: width=…}` for HTML must use **pixel** widths
   (e.g. `width=700`), not percentages; LaTeX may keep `width=5.5in`.
6. DESCRIPTION Hunspell “misspellings” (British English / domain terms)
   stay as written; explain them in `cran-comments.md` and keep jargon in
   `inst/WORDLIST` for `spelling` / aspell.
7. **Do not set `Additional_repositories` for CRAN or Bioconductor.**
   CRAN already treats the Bioconductor *software* repository as a
   standard dependency source; pointing it at
   `https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc` is redundant and
   triggers the incoming availability `? ?` NOTE. Use that field only
   for packages outside CRAN / Bioconductor / Omegahat / R-Forge.
8. **Optional visualisation dependencies** (e.g. `ComplexHeatmap`,
   `circlize`, `viridis`): put them in **Suggests**, not Imports, when
   they are used only by optional plotting helpers. Gate entry points
   with `requireNamespace(..., quietly = TRUE)` (Writing R Extensions),
   fail with an install hint via a small checker such as
   `.check_heatmap_dependencies()`, and wrap Rd `@examples` /
   testthat blocks in the same `requireNamespace` /
   `skip_if_not_installed()` pattern. Never call Suggests packages
   unconditionally from Imports-level code paths.

Excluding long Quarto articles from the tarball is justified when it
cuts rebuild time and Suggests risk; document the list in `.Rbuildignore`
and `cran-comments.md`, and never leave dangling relative links.

### GitHub and docs bootstrap (new packages)

Early setup via **usethis** (run once after creating the GitHub repo):

- `usethis::use_github_links()` — populate `DESCRIPTION` `URL` and
  `BugReports` from the GitHub remote (package site link, issues URL).
- `usethis::use_github_action()` — add r-lib Actions workflows; choose
  **1** (`check-standard`, R-CMD-check) and **2** (`test-coverage`).
  Extend or replace with repo-specific workflows (e.g. pkgdown) as the
  package matures; do not duplicate checks already covered elsewhere.
- `usethis::use_pkgdown_github_pages()` — scaffold `_pkgdown.yml`, a
  GitHub Pages deploy workflow, and `URL`/`BugReports` fields for a
  documented package site.

Customise the resulting pkgdown site per **pkgdown site customisation**
below; validate locally with `pkgdown::build_site()` before pushing.

### pkgdown site customisation

- Initiate GitHub Pages once with `quarto publish gh-pages` (from the
  repo root, not already on `gh-pages`). That creates the `gh-pages`
  branch and `_publish.yml` so later CI can deploy from the branch
  ([Quarto: Publish Action](https://quarto.org/docs/publishing/github-pages.html#publish-action)).
  For a project site (`https://<user>.github.io/<repo>/`) GitHub then
  publishes from `gh-pages` automatically; user/org sites
  (`https://<user>.github.io/`) still need **Settings → Pages** set to
  that branch.
- Prefer **external shared includes** under `pkgdown/` over raw HTML/JS
  embedded in `_pkgdown.yml` (e.g. `template.includes.after_body:
  after_body.html`). Use pkgdown’s `{{#site}}{{root}}{{/site}}` prefix
  for root-relative asset URLs.
- Put static assets in `pkgdown/assets/` (copied to the site root).
- Prefer **Sass** (`pkgdown/extra.scss`) over ad-hoc CSS for layout and
  theming (see [Slidecrafting SCSS](https://slidecrafting-book.com/scss)
  and [pkgdown customise](https://pkgdown.r-lib.org/articles/customise.html)).
- Keep the article TOC / reference sidebar collapsed to full width via
  SCSS unless a page truly needs an on-page TOC; tighten container
  gutters rather than inventing one-off inline styles.
- For wide embeds on the home page (network graph, slides), wrap in a
  shared CSS class (e.g. `.decovart-fullwidth`) and offer a full-screen
  link, mirroring the seminar-slides iframe pattern in `README.qmd`.
- Conditional CI: commit message containing `test coverage` triggers
  Codecov; `article generation` triggers `latexmk` for
  `article/main.tex`, uploads artifact `article-main-pdf`, and publishes
  `docs/article/main.pdf` on GitHub Pages.

### Package load hooks (`R/zzz.R`)

- Use `.onLoad()` for silent package options / registration.
- Use `.onAttach()` for a short welcome (`packageStartupMessage`),
  purpose of the package, main entry point, and notes on likely
  attach-order conflicts (e.g. `MASS::select` vs `dplyr::select`).
- Keep `_PACKAGE` docs in `R/DeCovarT-package.R`; regenerate PDF/HTML
  manuals with `scripts/auxiliary/generate_package_manual.R` when needed.

### Roxygen: `@export`, `@keywords internal`, `@noRd`, `@inheritParams`

- Every function with a substantive roxygen block (title / description /
  `@param` / `@return`) must be tagged with **`@export`** (and may also
  carry `@keywords internal` if it is an advanced / low-level helper).
  Documented functions are exported so `?fn` works after
  `devtools::document()` and so goodpractice / pkgcheck stay green.
- Use **`@noRd` if and only if** the object has **no** substantive
  roxygen documentation. Bare `@keywords internal` + `@noRd` is for tiny
  undocumented helpers only (e.g. `.onLoad`).
- Never use `@noRd` merely to hide docs you already wrote.
- Prefer **`@inheritParams`** when several functions share identical
  `@param` text (e.g. inherit `p` / `Sigma` / `y` /
  `mean_signature_matrix` from `loglik_multivariate`). Do not copy-paste
  the same parameter paragraphs across files.

## Air formatting and automated linting

This repo uses **[Air](https://posit-dev.github.io/air/)** (`air.toml`) and
**{lintr}** (`.lintr`), enforced via **pre-commit**
(`.pre-commit-config.yaml`) and VS Code format-on-save
(`Posit.air-vscode`).

- Format R with Air: line width **80**, indent **2 spaces**, space indent.
- Do not fight Air/lintr: match existing style after edits.
- Prefer Air over styler for formatting.
- **goodpractice / lintr (address these; ignore other lintr messages):**
  - Do not nest pipes inside other calls (`f(x |> g())`); assign first.
  - `expect_length(x, n)` rather than `expect_identical(length(x), n)`.
  - `expect_type(x, t)` rather than `expect_true(is.<type>(x))`.
  - `toString(x)` rather than `paste(x, collapse = ", ")`.
- Run / respect pre-commit hooks: `air-format`, `lintr` (warn-only),
  `parsable-R`, `no-browser-statement`, `use-tidy-description`,
  `deps-in-desc`, large-file check, `check-bibliography`. README sync
  is enforced in CI via `.github/workflows/render-readme.yaml`
  (`quarto render README.qmd`).
- Before any `pre-commit run` or commit, always:
  1. `pre-commit clean`
  2. Stage **all** intended files (`git add …`) — no leftover unstaged
     edits (avoids stash/autofix rollback failures)
  3. `pre-commit run` (or `--all-files`), then `git add -u` if hooks
     rewrote files, then commit
- Keep line length ≤ 80 characters.

## Coding style and naming conventions

**Naming**

- `snake_case` for variables, functions, and file names
  (e.g. `total_sales`, `clean_data()`).
- `UpperCamelCase` for R6, S3, S4, and S7 class names.
- `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants and global options.
- Prefix data-frame objects with `df_` when it aids clarity.
- Use verbs for functions; avoid ambiguous names (`customer_id`, not `id`).
- Do not shadow R builtins (`sd`, `data`, etc.).
- R6 private methods and fields start with `.` (e.g. `.get_data()` via
  `private$.get_data()`).
- Avoid one-off helpers unless they improve readability.

**Style**

- Follow the [tidyverse style guide](https://style.tidyverse.org/).
- Prefer `dplyr::mutate(..., .keep = "none")` over superseded
  `transmute()`.
- Use the native pipe `|>`.
- Use `package::function()` for non-base functions
  (e.g. `dplyr::select()`).
- Use `glue::glue()` for string interpolation in assigned strings;
  for `stop()` / `warning()` / `message()`, pass message parts as
  separate arguments to the condition function itself — never
  `stop(paste(...))`, `stop(paste0(...))`, or `stop(sprintf(...))`.
  Prefer `stop("a", " b=", x)` over wrapping `paste`.
- Assign with `<-`; use `=` only inside R6 methods.
- **Never use `<<-`** (or other super-assignment). For caches shared
  across calls, use a `local({ cache <- new.env(...); function(...) })`
  closure and assign with `cache$value <- ...`, not `cache <<- ...`.
- Never write `f(x <- value)`; assign first, then call the function.
- Handle missing values explicitly (`na.rm = TRUE`, `is.na()`).
- Preallocate vectors when filling in loops; avoid `c()` with a single
  argument (`c("x")` → `"x"`) and empty `c()` (prefer `NULL` or
  `numeric(0)` / `character(0)` with the right type).
- Prefer `!any(x)` over `all(!x)`, and `!all(x)` over `any(!x)`.
- Prefer `paste0()` over `paste(..., sep = "")`, and `file.path()` over
  `paste(..., sep = "/")`.
- In `grep()` / `grepl()` / `sub()` / `gsub()`, use `fixed = TRUE` when
  the pattern is a plain string, not a regular expression.
- Prefer `vapply(x, fun, type, ...)` / `purrr::map_*` with `fun` passed
  directly when an anonymous `\(x) fun(x)` wrapper adds nothing.
- Keep source lines ≤ **80** characters (Air / lintr); wrap prose and
  code rather than silencing the linter.
- Comment non-obvious logic only; prefer self-explanatory names.
- Prefer modular, reusable functions over long scripts.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies; use base R when clearly simpler.
  Check existing package helpers before adding new code.
- Save ggplot2 figures with `ggplot2::ggsave()`, not base
  `grDevices` devices, unless TikZ/LaTeX export is required.

### dplyr programming (indirection)

When programming with dplyr (especially **inside package functions**),
follow
[Programming with dplyr — Indirection](https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/articles/programming.html#indirection):

- Data-masking argument from the caller: **embrace** with `{{ var }}`.
- Character names / loops / Shiny inputs: use the `.data` pronoun,
  e.g. `.data[[var]]`, `.data[["column"]]`.
- Tidy-select with character vectors: `dplyr::all_of()` / `any_of()`.
- Dynamic column names on the LHS: use `:=` with glue / embracing.
- Prefer explicit `.data[["col"]]` inside package code over bare names
  when that avoids NSE ambiguity.

### Function signatures and ellipsis

- For wrappers around other package functions, **forward all relevant
  arguments explicitly**, including `...` when the upstream API uses it.
- Document every argument in roxygen; do not leave undocumented `...`
  that silently swallow typos.
- If the user omits optional arguments, pass through or rely on the
  **upstream package defaults**—do not invent silent overrides unless
  documented.
- Prefer named arguments over positional ones at call sites.

**Tidyverse preferences (when needed):**

- `stringr` for text; `lubridate` for dates; `forcats` for factors.
- `dplyr` / `tidyr` / `tibble` for tabular work in this package
  (match existing style; use `data.table` only when speed clearly needs it).

## Vignettes and Quarto authoring

- Prefer Quarto for reproducible reports and vignettes combining code
  and results; British English; precise, cited scientific prose.
- Document pre-processing of inputs and non-obvious numerical /
  mathematical devices (ALR, analytic gradients, constrained optimisers)
  when they matter for understanding or speed.
- Keep vignettes reproducible; avoid duplicating the full Article word
  budget—cross-link the paper and package help pages.
- Apply the editorial rules under **Scientific writing** whenever a
  vignette presents scientific claims, methods, or benchmarks.

### Quarto Markdown checklist (`.qmd` / `.Rmd` / `.md`)

Prefer constructs that render cleanly in HTML / pkgdown, stay readable
in source, and keep the RStudio / VS Code **Visual Editor** usable.
Follow Quarto
[cross-references](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/cross-references.html)
for figures, tables, equations, sections, and callouts.

- **Cross-references:** label figures / tables / equations / sections
  (`#fig-…`, `#tbl-…`, `#eq-…`, `{#sec-…}`) and cite them mid-sentence
  (`see @fig-…`, `see @eq-…`), never wrapped as `(@fig-…)`. Across
  vignettes use pkgdown HTML links
  (e.g. `[synthetic scenarios](synthetic-scenarios.html#sec-…)`).
- **Lists:** only the basic Quarto / Pandoc forms documented at
  [Markdown Basics — Lists](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/markdown-basics.html#lists)
  — unordered (`-`, `*`, `+`) and ordered (`1.`, `2.`, …), nested as
  documented. **Forbidden:** Pandoc example lists (`(@)`, `(@label)`,
  or any hard-wrap where a line starts with `@label)`), task /
  checkbox lists (`- [ ]`, `- [x]`), and unicode step markers
  (`- ① …`). Example lists block Visual Mode (“Document contains
  example lists…”).
- **Package / method names:** put **package**, **software tool**, and
  **named method** labels in inline code spans (`` `limma` ``,
  `` `edgeR` ``, `` `DESeq2` ``, `` `AutoGeneS` ``, `` `INDEED` ``,
  `` `MixSim` ``, `` `SCTransform` ``). Keep citation keys (`@R-limma`)
  and maths unchanged. Do not backtick generic statistical terms
  (ANOVA, Gini coefficient, nested CV). Mermaid node labels may keep
  short plain names.
- **Warnings / notes:** prefer labelled Quarto callouts with
  cross-references (`::: {#nte-foo .callout-warning}` … `:::` then
  `@nte-foo`) over raw blockquotes with pasted glyphs. See
  [callout cross-references](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/cross-references.html#callouts).
- **Emoji / icons:** use GitHub-style `:name:` shortcodes from
  [rxaviers/emoji-cheat-sheet](https://gist.github.com/rxaviers/7360908)
  (`:warning:`, `:information_source:`), not literal unicode emoji in
  source. Enable Quarto emoji support in YAML when they must render
  (`from: markdown+emoji`).
- **Videos:** embed with the
  [video shortcode](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/videos.html)
  (`{{< video URL title="…" aria-label="…" start="20" >}}`); keep
  `title`, `aria-label`, and `start` as **separate** attributes. For a
  numbered figure wrap it in a `::: {#fig-…}` div whose **last
  paragraph is the caption**, then cite `@fig-…`. HTML embeds a player;
  other formats degrade to a link.
- **Linked figures:** when a local image comes from a website, use
  Quarto’s
  [linked-figure](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/figures.html#linked-figures)
  form `[![caption](local-path){#fig-label}](https://source-url)` —
  `{#fig-…}` on the **image**, not the outer link. Prefer this when a
  stable public URL exists; never invent URLs.
- Keep Visual Editor–hostile syntax out of vignettes unless a feature
  (callouts, shortcodes) is intentionally required and documented here.

### Tables: tinytable (static) vs reactable (interactive)

- **Static tables** in vignettes, pkgdown articles, and other Quarto
  HTML: use `tinytable::tt()` by default. Pin `tinytable (>= 0.19.0)`
  in Suggests once that CRAN release ships the fix for
  [tinytable#687](https://github.com/vincentarelbundock/tinytable/issues/687)
  ([#688](https://github.com/vincentarelbundock/tinytable/pull/688));
  until then install from GitHub for local / rhub checks
  (`remotes::install_github("vincentarelbundock/tinytable")`). Do not
  introduce `knitr::kable()`, `kableExtra`, or `flextable` for new
  static tables unless the user asks. For maths in cells write
  `$\\kappa_2$`-style strings (double backslashes) per the
  [tinytable math docs](https://vincentarelbundock.github.io/tinytable/vignettes/format.html#math)
  and set `options(tinytable_html_mathjax = TRUE)` in setup when HTML
  needs inline MathJax. Quarto Markdown pipe tables remain acceptable
  for very simple tables without styling or cell maths.
- **Reveal.js slides / interactive HTML:** prefer `reactable` (see
  **Slide design**).

### Mermaid rendering constraints (README, vignettes, slides)

- Do **not** use plain code fences like ```` ``` mermaid ```` for figures
  intended to render in Quarto outputs; use executable Mermaid chunks.
  Minimal pattern:
  - opening fence: ```` ```{mermaid} ````
  - options: `%%| label: fig-example` and
    `%%| fig-cap: "Example caption."`
  - diagram body: e.g. `flowchart TD` then `A --> B`
  - closing fence: ```` ``` ````
- Cross-reference Mermaid figures with Quarto labels (`@fig-...`), as
  documented at
  <https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/diagrams.html#cross-references>.
- Set Mermaid theme to **sandstone** for consistency unless a context
  explicitly requires another documented theme.
- For reveal.js decks, keep Mermaid configured under `format: revealjs:`
  and verify rendering in both `quarto preview` and `quarto render`.
- For PDF/Word paths using Mermaid, use the installed
  `chrome-headless-shell` backend and verify rendered output rather than
  raw diagram source.

**HTML: test native Quarto Mermaid before any JS fallback.** Empty
figure boxes on the web almost always mean the `{mermaid}` engine never
ran. **Do not** add a client-side fallback first.

1. Set `execute.eval: true` in `_quarto.yml` (or `%%| eval: true` on the
   Mermaid chunk). `eval: false` skips the diagram engine and leaves an
   empty `<div>` where the figure should be.
2. `quarto render` the HTML and inspect the output: a healthy diagram is
   a `<pre class="mermaid mermaid-js">` block **containing the flowchart
   source**, plus Quarto’s own
   `site_libs/quarto-diagram/mermaid.min.js` and
   `site_libs/quarto-diagram/mermaid-init.js` in the page `<head>`.
3. Open the page in a browser (`quarto preview` or the built HTML). The
   `pre.mermaid` block should become an SVG. That is the supported path
   for Quarto books and vignettes.

Only if those `pre.mermaid` blocks are present **and still unrendered**
in the browser (e.g. pkgdown README fences that Quarto never compiled)
may you add the optional DeCovarT client-side fallback:
`pkgdown/assets/mermaid-init.js` plus the CDN include in `_pkgdown.yml`
(`after_body`), or an equivalent `html/mermaid-after-body.html`. If
native `eval: true` already emits `pre.mermaid` + quarto-diagram, **do
not** add that fallback — remove it.

## Performance and optimisation

- Profile with `profvis` before optimising.
- Prefer vectorised code, the apply family, or `purrr` over explicit loops.
- Use `data.table` for large in-memory tables when speed matters.
- Use `duckdb` for out-of-memory or SQL-style queries.
- CSV/TSV: `readr::read_csv()` / `readr::read_tsv()` in tidyverse
  workflows (`show_col_types = FALSE`); `data.table::fread()` otherwise.
- Avoid copying large objects unnecessarily.
- Document non-obvious speed-ups (analytic derivatives, tensor tricks,
  constrained reparametrisations) in vignettes / article.

## Testing and validation

Follow
[R Packages — Designing your test suite](https://r-pkgs.org/testing-design.html):

- Use **testthat** edition 3 (`Config/testthat/edition: 3`).
- Create tests with **`usethis::use_test("file_stem")`** so that
  `R/foo.R` maps to `tests/testthat/test-foo.R` (this repo mirrors
  numeric stems, e.g. `R/03_03_….R` → `test-03_03_….R`).
- Tests should be **self-sufficient** and **self-contained**: prefer
  almost only `test_that()` calls; minimise top-level file scope.
- Focus on external behaviour, edge cases, missing values, and known
  bug regressions—not every trivial line.
- Cover every exported function with at least a smoke or correctness
  test (except explicitly deferred APIs, e.g. experimental MAP helpers).
- testthat style (goodpractice / lintr):
  - Prefer `expect_identical()` over `expect_equal()` when comparison
    should be exact (types, integers, names, dims).
  - Use `expect_equal()` only when tolerance / numeric noise matters.
  - Prefer `expect_named(x, expected)` over
    `expect_identical(names(x), expected)`.
  - `expect_length(x, n)` rather than `expect_identical(length(x), n)`.
  - `expect_type(x, t)` rather than `expect_true(is.<type>(x))`.
  - Split `expect_true(a && b)` into separate `expect_true(a)` and
    `expect_true(b)` calls.
- Use `set.seed()` for stochastic tests; store fixtures under
  `tests/testthat/fixtures/` when needed.
- Interactive: `devtools::test()`, `devtools::test_coverage()`,
  `devtools::test_coverage_active_file()`.
- Package checks: run `devtools::check()` **and**
  `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()` (rOpenSci
  [pkgcheck](https://docs.ropensci.org/pkgcheck/)); fix failing
  checks before proceeding (`:eyes:` items are optional). Optionally
  write a markdown report with `pkgcheck::checks_to_markdown()`.
- CI: keep `test-coverage` and `R-CMD-check` workflows meaningful
  (pkgcheck matches workflow run names against `cmd|coverage`).

## Spell checking (`spelling`)

Treat Hunspell as a regular package check, not a one-off. Setup:

```r
spelling::spell_check_setup(".", lang = "en-GB", error = FALSE)
```

That adds `spelling` to Suggests, `Language: en-GB` in `DESCRIPTION`,
`tests/spelling.R`, `inst/WORDLIST`, and `.aspell/defaults.R`.

- Keep **`error = FALSE`**: `R CMD check` prints suspects but does not
  fail on residual jargon or LaTeX tokens.
- **`inst/WORDLIST`**: proper nouns (Aitchison, Becht), method/package
  names, and domain terms Hunspell misses (pseudobulk, ALR). After
  `update_wordlist()`, prune LaTeX (`mathbb`, `Biggl`), math indices
  (`gi`, `jj`), and URL crumbs (`www`, `com`). Fix real typos and
  Americanisms in the source (artefact, not artifact).
- **Commit `.aspell/`**; do not gitignore or `.Rbuildignore` it. R
  treats it as a known package directory so CRAN aspell
  (`_R_CHECK_CRAN_INCOMING_USE_ASPELL_`) reuses `inst/WORDLIST`.

## Reproducibility and collaboration

- Capture environment with `sessioninfo::session_info()`.
- Pin packages with `renv` when used; otherwise lock via DESCRIPTION /
  CI.
- Maintain a clear README (goals, installation, pipeline Mermaid,
  badges, usage): edit `README.qmd`, then `quarto render README.qmd`
  (CI verifies / re-renders via
  `.github/workflows/render-readme.yaml`).
- Use descriptive commit messages and feature branches.
- Track user-facing changes in `NEWS.md`.
- Share results via HTML/PDF (pkgdown, Quarto) as appropriate.

## Shiny (if used)

- Use modules (`moduleServer`, `NS()`).
- Keep UI declarative; separate data logic.
- Session state in `reactiveValues` / `session$userData`, not globals.
- Static assets in `www/`.
- Avoid `renderUI` / `uiOutput` unless necessary.

## Advanced practices

- Prefer **R6** for complex mutable objects when appropriate; S3/S4/S7
  when idiomatic (this package currently leans on functional APIs).
- Automate heavy pipelines with `targets` when worthwhile.
- Use Docker and CI/CD (GitHub Actions) for checks and pkgdown.

## Preferred R dependencies

| Task | Package |
|------|---------|
| Lists / functional code | `purrr` |
| Paths | `here`, `fs` (avoid ad-hoc `normalizePath()`) |
| Tabular data | `dplyr`, `tidyr`, `tibble` (`data.table` if needed) |
| Out-of-memory data | `duckdb` |
| Plotting | `ggplot2`; heatmaps: `ComplexHeatmap` (Suggests + `requireNamespace`); interactive: `ggiraph` |
| Static tables (vignettes / Quarto HTML) | `tinytable` |
| Slide / HTML interactive tables | `reactable` |
| Discrete ggplot scales | named vectors via `stats::setNames()` |
| Input checks | `checkmate` |
| Messages | `cli` |
| Strings | `glue`, `stringr` |
| Parallelism | `mirai` (or existing package patterns) |
| JSON | `jsonlite`; large JSON: `yyjsonr` |
| C++ integration | `Rcpp` |
| Web apps | `shiny` |
| Reproducibility | `renv`, Quarto |
| Package workflow | `devtools`, `usethis`, `roxygen2`, `testthat` |
| Formatting / lint | Air, `lintr`, pre-commit |
| Large raw data | DVC |

Match `DESCRIPTION` Imports when adding package code; do not introduce
unused Suggests/Imports without need.

---

# Bibliography

DeCovarT keeps **two separate citation streams**. Do not mix them.

## A. Literature (Zotero → citation keys → Git)

**Source of truth** for papers / books / theses is the author's
[Zotero library](https://www.zotero.org/bastienchassagnol/library).
Do **not** invent BibTeX entries, citekeys, or DOIs; add the item in
Zotero first, then export. Missing metadata may be completed only via
the deterministic Crossref enrichment CLI below (never by unconstrained
LLM guessing).

From **Zotero 8 onward** (this machine runs **Zotero 9**), citation keys
are a first-class field in `zotero.sqlite`
(`fields.fieldName = 'citationKey'`), so the built-in BibTeX exporter is
enough: export into `inst/REFERENCES.bib` /
`article/decovart_library.bib`, then run
`python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean`.
Set the key formula once under Edit → Settings → Cite → Citation Key.
Copy or open the DB with `immutable=1` when the desktop app holds a lock.

### Zotcurate (`zotc`) — collection curation from documents

Use **[zotcurate](https://github.com/jeetsukumaran/zotcurate)** to bridge
`.bib` / `.qmd` / CSV citation keys and Zotero collections. Prefer **CSV**
for tabular outputs; prefer **YAML** for collection-hierarchy snapshots.

```bash
sudo chown -R "$(whoami):$(whoami)" ~/.local ~/.zotc ~/.venvs   # once if needed
bash scripts/install_zotcurate_home.sh
```

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `~/.local/bin/zotc` | Wrapper with `-z ~/Zotero/zotero.sqlite` |
| `~/.zotc/*` | Credentials (chmod 600; never commit) |
| `~/Zotero/zotero.sqlite` | Desktop DB + native citation keys |

Library ID: `7172053`.

**Collection membership (leaf only):** assign **exactly one** collection —
the **most specific leaf** matching the paper (e.g.
`…/3) feature selection: one-vs-all/ii) multivariate approaches`).
Do **not** also attach the parent or higher ancestors. Prefer one
accurate leaf over parent+child duplicates.

**Inputs for missing-citation checks:** `.bib`, `.tex`, `.qmd` (and CSV
for collection sync). Ad-hoc collection exports such as
`IV) build signature matrix.bib` are merge sources only — keep them
gitignored at the repo root; after merging run `--clean`.

**Both directions:**

1. **Zotero → Git:** export → `check_bibliography.py --clean` (article
   copy is the `--clean` upstream when both files exist).
2. **Git/docs → Zotero:** `zotc keys extract` then
   `collection add|create` (dry-run first); new items via Web API /
   desktop → **leaf only** → native citekey → re-export → `--clean`.
3. Software tools → `packages.bib`, not Zotero literature.

| Path | Consumer |
|------|----------|
| `article/decovart_library.bib` | LaTeX manuscript |
| `inst/REFERENCES.bib` | Quarto vignettes (literature half) |

- Exactly these two literature copies (independent, kept in sync).
- After every Zotero export: `python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean`
  (allowed fields only; DOI-dedupe; sync both copies).
- Allowed fields (whitelist enforced by `scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py`):
  `title`, `author`, `editor`, `year`, `journal`, `booktitle`,
  `publisher`, `howpublished`, `doi`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`,
  `isbn`, `issn`, `edition`, `school`, `institution`, `url`.
  Strip everything else.
- Required fields by type (Rdpack / `utils::bibentry`):
  - `@article` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `journal`
  - `@book` — `title`, `year`, `publisher`, plus `author` **or** `editor`
  - `@incollection` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `booktitle`, `publisher`
  - `@phdthesis` / `@mastersthesis` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `school`
  - `@techreport` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `institution`
- Pre-commit hook `check-bibliography` validates literature hygiene and
  `article/main.tex` citekeys (against literature **and** `packages.bib`).

### Completing missing fields (Crossref + bibtex-tidy)

When BibTeX / Rdpack validation fails because metadata is incomplete:

1. Enrich incomplete entries with the repo CLI (DOI-first, then
   title/author/year Crossref search; merge **missing fields only**;
   never overwrite existing values; never invent DOIs):

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/bib_enrich.py \
  inst/REFERENCES.bib --write --backup \
  --min-score 92 --report .cursor/bib-enrichment.json
```

2. Review `.cursor/bib-enrichment.json` and the git diff. Reject records
   marked `ambiguous`. Manually verify any `unresolved` / `partial`
   entries (prefer Zotero correction, then re-export).
3. Normalise formatting with `bibtex-tidy`:

```bash
npx bibtex-tidy@1.14.0 inst/REFERENCES.bib \
  --modify --curly --numeric --sort=key
```

If `bibtex-tidy@latest` is stable on your machine, prefer latest. If it
crashes (e.g. current Node 22 edge cases), pin to `@1.14.0` and/or
upgrade Node.js to the latest LTS release.

4. Sync both literature copies and validate:

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py
```

Install once (user-level with `uv`, no sudo; keeps tooling in home
directory instead of this repository):

```bash
uv venv ~/.venvs/bib-enrich
uv pip install --python ~/.venvs/bib-enrich/bin/python \
  bibtexparser httpx rapidfuzz typer rich
~/.venvs/bib-enrich/bin/python -c \
  "import bibtexparser, httpx, rapidfuzz, typer, rich; print('OK')"
# then run:
# python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/bib_enrich.py ...
# bibtex-tidy via npx; prefer latest Node.js LTS
```

GitHub issues — DOI bridge:

```markdown
- [ ] [Paper title](https://doi.org/10.xxxx/xxxxx) — `@citekey`
```

## B. Software / tools (`packages.bib` — not Zotero)

R, Bioconductor, and similar **software** citations are generated
automatically into a standalone bibliography — **not** stored in Zotero.

| Path | Consumer |
|------|----------|
| `inst/packages.bib` | Quarto vignettes (software half) |
| `article/packages.bib` | LaTeX (synced copy) |

Regenerate (from repository root):

```bash
Rscript scripts/curate_bibliography/generate_packages_bib.R
```

That script discovers packages used in **`vignettes/`** and **`article/`**
only (CRANpkg macros, `library()` / `pkg::`, plus an explicit software
allowlist for prose-only mentions such as `huge`). It does **not** dump
DESCRIPTION Imports/Suggests or the whole package dependency tree.

## C. Quarto vignette YAML (always two bibliographies)

Every `vignettes/*.qmd` must declare **both** files:

```yaml
bibliography:
  - ../inst/packages.bib
  - ../inst/REFERENCES.bib
link-citations: true
```

Example citations: literature `@wuEstimatingHeterogeneousGene2022`;
software `@R-huge`, `@R-BDgraph`, `@R-base`.

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --check-qmd
```

